  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 7175397376
    m_Localized: Ocean Mysteries
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 15593989201920
    m_Localized: Anglerfish
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 15691867480064
    m_Localized: Bioluminescent Jelly
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 15820888465408
    m_Localized: Clownfish
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 15897677783040
    m_Localized: Crab
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 15925100142592
    m_Localized: Electric Eel
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 15952698662912
    m_Localized: Blue Whale
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 15993601515520
    m_Localized: Jellyfish
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16032138780672
    m_Localized: Lionfish
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16052615372800
    m_Localized: Remora
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16073163268096
    m_Localized: Sea Dragon
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16089365864448
    m_Localized: Sea Turtle
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16129316610048
    m_Localized: Sea Urchin
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16189945274368
    m_Localized: Seahorse
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 16223143190528
    m_Localized: Stingray
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 25058125799424
    m_Localized: Laboratory
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 93635747971072
    m_Localized: "This deep-sea predator uses a glowing lure to catch its food in
      the dark!\nThe anglerfish lives in the pitch-black ocean depths. It has a built-in
      fishing rod on its head with a glowing tip called a bioluminescent lure, which
      it wiggles to attract curious prey \u2014 then SNAP! Dinner is served."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 93695680380928
    m_Localized: "This jelly lives deep in the ocean where it's always dark. To survive,
      it glows in the dark using a special power called bioluminescence.\nIt flashes
      its light to scare predators, find friends, or attract tiny food.\nIts glow
      comes from tiny light-making chemicals in its body\u2014like a living nightlight!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 93754048315392
    m_Localized: "Clownfish live in a special partnership with sea anemones\u2014stinging
      creatures that protect the clownfish from danger.\nBut guess what? The clownfish
      doesn\u2019t get stung! It has a slimy coat that keeps it safe.\nThey clean
      the anemone, and the anemone keeps predators away.\nBest friends forever under
      the sea!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 93782888349696
    m_Localized: "The Armored Scuttler!\nCrabs wear their skeleton on the outside
      like armor\u2014this is called an exoskeleton!\nThey move sideways using their
      strong legs and use their claws to grab food or defend themselves.\nWhen they
      grow too big for their shell, they shed it and grow a new one.\nIt\u2019s like
      getting a brand new suit of armor!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 93809790615552
    m_Localized: "Nature\u2019s Battery!\nThe electric eel can produce up to 600
      volts of electricity\u2014enough to stun prey or scare off predators!\nIt uses
      this power to hunt in dark, muddy waters, where eyesight doesn\u2019t help
      much.\nEven though it\u2019s called an eel, it\u2019s actually more like a
      knifefish.\nIt \u201Ctalks\u201D to other eels with small zaps\u2014an underwater
      Morse code!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 93841621188608
    m_Localized: "The blue whale is the largest animal on Earth \u2014 even bigger
      than a bus!\nDespite its massive size, it feeds on tiny creatures called krill,
      swallowing thousands in a single gulp.\nIt communicates using deep, powerful
      sounds that can travel for hundreds of kilometers underwater.\nThough it looks
      huge, the blue whale is a calm and graceful traveler of the ocean."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 93862701760512
    m_Localized: "The Floating Mystery!\nJellyfish have been drifting through oceans
      for over 500 million years\u2014long before dinosaurs!\nThey have no brain,
      heart, or bones, yet they move and catch food with their stinging tentacles.\nThey
      glow, pulse, and float like ocean ghosts, riding the currents with ease.\nSome
      are tiny, some are huge\u2014but all are mesmerizing wonders of the deep."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 93886022090752
    m_Localized: "Beautiful but Dangerous!\nThe lionfish is covered in bright stripes
      and long, flowing spines\u2014like a lion\u2019s mane underwater!\nBut don\u2019t
      be fooled\u2014those spines are venomous, and they help scare away predators.\nIt
      hunts with lightning-fast strikes and is known for being a stealthy invader
      in coral reefs.\nLooks like royalty, hunts like a ninja!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 93905345249280
    m_Localized: "The Ocean Hitchhiker!\nThe remora has a suction pad on top of its
      head\u2014like a natural sticky cup!\nIt uses it to stick to sharks, whales,
      and turtles, getting free rides through the ocean.\nWhile traveling, it eats
      leftover scraps and keeps its host clean.\nIt\u2019s the ultimate ride-share
      buddy of the sea!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 93938006294528
    m_Localized: "The Living Leaf!\nSea dragons are relatives of seahorses, and they
      look like floating seaweed to stay safe from predators.\nTheir leafy fins don\u2019t
      help them swim fast\u2014they're for camouflage, not speed.\nThey drift gently
      through the water, blending into coral and kelp like underwater ninjas.\nIt\u2019s
      not a mythical dragon\u2014but it\u2019s just as magical!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 93960068333568
    m_Localized: "The Ocean Traveler!\nSea turtles can live for over 50 years and
      travel thousands of kilometers across the ocean.\nThey always return to the
      same beach where they were born to lay their eggs\u2014like they have a built-in
      GPS!\nWith their strong flippers, they glide gracefully through the water like
      flying birds.\nThey help keep the ocean healthy by eating jellyfish and seagrass\u2014they\u2019re
      nature\u2019s cleanup crew!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 93987922706432
    m_Localized: "The Ocean\u2019s Spiky Ball!\nSea urchins look like little balls
      covered in sharp spines, but those spines help them move, protect, and clean
      themselves!\nThey crawl slowly across rocks using tiny tube feet hidden underneath.\nWith
      a mouth on the bottom called \u201CAristotle\u2019s lantern,\u201D they scrape
      algae off surfaces\u2014like ocean lawnmowers!\nSome even wear seashells and
      rocks as camouflage hats!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 94010622279680
    m_Localized: "Tails & Tiny Miracles!\nSeahorses are one of the only animals where
      the male gives birth!\nThe dad carries the babies in a special pouch until
      they\u2019re ready to swim free.\nThey use their curly tails to anchor onto
      plants so they don\u2019t drift away in currents.\nWith eyes that move independently,
      they watch for food and danger at the same time\u2014like tiny underwater robots!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 94027932172288
    m_Localized: "The Gliding Shadow!\nStingrays flap their wide fins like wings,
      letting them glide smoothly across the ocean floor.\nThey often hide under
      sand, using their flat bodies for camouflage.\nTheir tail has a sharp, venomous
      barb\u2014used only for defense!\nWith eyes on top and a mouth underneath,
      they\u2019re built for sneaky snacking on clams and crustaceans."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 277737842401280
    m_Localized: Seaweed Strip
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 277867811299328
    m_Localized: "Seaweed might look like a plant, but it's actually algae! It lives
      in shallow water and soaks up sunlight to make food \u2014 just like trees
      do on land. Some types of seaweed are even used in sushi and ice c"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 277908659625984
    m_Localized: Kelp Leaf
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 277936929234944
    m_Localized: "Kelp is the giant of the algae world. It can grow over 60 centimeters
      (2 feet) every single day! Kelp forests are like underwater jungles \u2014
      they provide food and hiding places for otters, fish, and sea snails."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 277954998296576
    m_Localized: Phytoplankton Drop
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 277970479472640
    m_Localized: "Don\u2019t let their tiny size fool you! Phytoplankton float near
      the surface of the ocean and produce more than half the oxygen we breathe.
      Without them, animals (and people!) would run out of air."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 277982173192192
    m_Localized: Red Algae Thread
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278005418024960
    m_Localized: Red algae love warm, sunny oceans and come in bright colors. Humans
      use them to make jelly and toothpaste! Some red algae even live deeper than
      other plants because they absorb blue light best.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278022803415040
    m_Localized: Seagrass Blade
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278036271325184
    m_Localized: Seagrass is a real flowering plant that lives underwater. It grows
      in huge green meadows and helps protect baby fish and turtles. Its roots hold
      the ocean floor together, like grass on land holds the dirt.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278057070878720
    m_Localized: Sargassum Leaf
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278073470607360
    m_Localized: "Sargassum floats on the ocean surface in golden-brown mats. Tiny
      fish, shrimp, and even baby turtles use it as a floating home. Some boats even
      get stuck in it because there\u2019s so much!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278149341372416
    m_Localized: Fish Scale
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278183822745600
    m_Localized: "Fish scales grow in layers as the fish gets older \u2014 just like
      the rings inside a tree! Scientists can tell a fish\u2019s age by looking closely
      at a scale under a microscope."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278198687358976
    m_Localized: Jellyfish Tentacle
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278231298072576
    m_Localized: "Jellyfish don\u2019t have a brain or bones, but they\u2019ve survived
      for over 500 million years. Some jellyfish have stingers powerful enough to
      scare away predators \u2014 or even stop a fish in its tracks!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278248985452544
    m_Localized: Crab Claw Fragment
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278265490038784
    m_Localized: "Crabs grow by shedding their old shell in a process called molting.
      Right after molting, their new shell is soft \u2014 so they have to hide until
      it hardens again!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278279641620480
    m_Localized: Octopus Ink Sample
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278296485945344
    m_Localized: "When threatened, octopuses shoot out a cloud of dark ink to confuse
      predators. Some ink even contains chemicals that dull the predator\u2019s sense
      of smell!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278366400798720
    m_Localized: Dolphin Echo Ping
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278387380707328
    m_Localized: "Dolphins don\u2019t rely on sight \u2014 they use echolocation!
      They send out sound waves and listen to the echoes bouncing back to \u201Csee\u201D
      the shape and distance of things underwater."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278408012488704
    m_Localized: Shark Tooth
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278419777511424
    m_Localized: "Sharks constantly lose teeth \u2014 and that\u2019s okay! They
      grow new ones in rows like a conveyor belt, replacing old teeth every few days
      to weeks depending on the species."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278440442847232
    m_Localized: Black Sand Grain
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278521455828992
    m_Localized: "Black sand is made from cooled lava! It\u2019s mostly found on
      volcanic beaches and is rich in minerals like basalt and magnetite."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278535200563200
    m_Localized: Manganese Nodule
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278547817029632
    m_Localized: "These metal-rich lumps grow on the deep ocean floor \u2014 but
      very slowly. It can take millions of years for one nodule to grow just a few
      centimeters!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278569509969920
    m_Localized: Salt Crystal
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278585129558016
    m_Localized: "Ocean salt forms naturally when seawater evaporates under the sun.
      It\u2019s the same kind of salt that flavors your food \u2014 but it starts
      in the sea!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278601256656896
    m_Localized: Pumice Rock
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278623629074432
    m_Localized: Pumice is a type of volcanic rock full of tiny holes, which makes
      it so light it can float on water!
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278638086840320
    m_Localized: Deep Sea Sediment
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278657116397568
    m_Localized: "This fine, dusty layer forms over time as tiny dead plankton and
      particles settle on the ocean floor \u2014 creating a record of ocean history."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278669699309568
    m_Localized: Limestone Chip
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278692109475840
    m_Localized: Limestone is often formed from the compacted shells of ancient sea
      creatures. Many coral reefs and sea caves are made of limestone.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278719871574016
    m_Localized: Glowing Algae Drop
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278777878798336
    m_Localized: "Some algae can glow in the dark! This magical-looking light is
      called bioluminescence, and it\u2019s caused by a chemical reaction when the
      water is disturbed \u2014 like from a wave or a swimming fish."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278793527746560
    m_Localized: Ancient Coin
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278811521310720
    m_Localized: "This coin may have come from a shipwreck long ago. Oceans hold
      secrets from ancient travelers, pirates, and lost civilizations \u2014 all
      buried beneath the waves."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278823047258112
    m_Localized: Message in a Bottle
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278840235515904
    m_Localized: "People have sent messages in bottles across oceans for hundreds
      of years. Some are found decades later on faraway shores \u2014 carrying stories,
      coordinates, or cries for help."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278853741174784
    m_Localized: Plastic Fragment
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278870686162944
    m_Localized: "Tiny pieces of plastic, called microplastics, are found in oceans
      all over the world. Sea animals often mistake them for food \u2014 it\u2019s
      one of the biggest threats to ocean health today."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278881192894464
    m_Localized: Ghost Net Fiber
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278897290633216
    m_Localized: Ghost nets are abandoned fishing nets that drift through the sea.
      They trap fish, turtles, and even dolphins by accident. Cleaning them up helps
      protect marine life.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278916760592384
    m_Localized: Ocean Map Scrap
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278935282638848
    m_Localized: This torn piece of map might lead to something mysterious. Explorers
      and scientists use underwater maps to discover new trenches, ridges, and even
      ancient ruins hidden below.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278971974410240
    m_Localized: Clam Shell
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 278993021427712
    m_Localized: "Clams spend most of their lives buried in sand, quietly filtering
      seawater to eat. Some clams can live more than 100 years \u2014 that\u2019s
      older than most people!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 279009526013952
    m_Localized: Sea Urchin Spine
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 279024155746304
    m_Localized: Sea urchins might look like prickly balls, but they move around
      using hundreds of tiny tube feet. Their spines help protect them from hungry
      fish and curious crabs.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 279038856781824
    m_Localized: Starfish Arm Tip
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 279051435499520
    m_Localized: "Starfish don\u2019t just lose arms \u2014 they grow them back!
      Some starfish can even regrow an entire body from just one arm, as long as
      part of the center remains."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 279071253585920
    m_Localized: Coral Fragment
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 279088307625984
    m_Localized: "Coral reefs may look like colorful rocks, but they\u2019re actually
      made by tiny animals called polyps. Each polyp builds a hard shell, and together
      they form coral colonies \u2014 homes for thousands of ocean creatures."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 279106825478144
    m_Localized: Sea Cucumber Skin
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 279123808215040
    m_Localized: "Sea cucumbers slowly crawl across the ocean floor, cleaning it
      as they go. They suck up sand and digest nutrients, leaving the ocean cleaner
      behind them \u2014 like little underwater vacuum cleaners!"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 279136017833984
    m_Localized: Snail Shell
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 279147510226944
    m_Localized: Ocean snails carry their homes on their backs. As they move, they
      leave winding trails through the sand, and some even drill into other shells
      to eat what's inside
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds: []
